Railroad executives invented modern methods for running large-scale business operations, creating a blueprint that all large corporations basically followed. <!--They created career tracks that took 18-year-old boys and turned them into brakemen, conductors and engineers.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Licht |first=Walter |url={{google books|plainurl=y|id=183_AwAAQBAJ}} |title=Working for the Railroad: The Organization of Work in the Nineteenth Century |date=2014-07-14 |publisher=Princeton University Press |isbn=978-1-4008-5584-1 |language=en}}</ref></ref>--> They were first to encounter managerial complexities, labor union issues, and problems of geographical competition. Railroads had to manage safety, traffic and freight over large geographic areas and had to keep track of railcars, which could go missing for months. Railroads also made extensive use of telegraph communications.<ref name="Chandler 1993">
{{cite book |title = The Visible Hand: The Management Revolution in American Business
