====Spending====
Government spending increased from 8.0% of GNP under [[Herbert Hoover]] in 1932 to 10.2% of GNP in 1936. [[Franklin D. Roosevelt]] balanced the "regular" budget the emergency budget was funded by debt, which increased from 33.6% of GNP in 1932 to 40.9% in 1936. Deficit spending had been recommended by some economists, most notably the Briton [[John Maynard Keynes]]. Roosevelt met Keynes but did not pay attention to his recommendations. After a meeting with Keynes, who kept drawing diagrams, Roosevelt remarked that "He must be a mathematician rather than a political economist". John Keynes' approach to the Great Depression could have been a solution. His method was to keep the feds spending as much as they could even on random purchases but the money had to keep moving. It is believed that if America went through with John Keynes plan the Great Depression could have been avoided entirely. The Feds started to spend more when President Roosevelt came into office as the federal government doubled income tax rates in 1932. Total government tax revenues as a percentage of GDP shot up from 10.8% in 1929 to 16.6% in 1933. Higher tax rates tended to reduce consumption and aggregate demand. Spending would go up and America would get out of the great depression when World War II happened after [[Attack on Pearl Harbor|Japan's attack on American forces in Pearl Harbor]] in December 1941 led to much sharper increases in government purchases, and the economy pushed quickly into an inflationary gap.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Moggridge |first=Donald Edward |title=Maynard Keynes: An Economist's Biography |year=1992 |publisher=Routledge |location=New York |isbn=978-0-415-05141-5 |page=[https://archive.org/details/maynardkeyneseco0047mogg/page/582 582] |url=https://archive.org/details/maynardkeyneseco0047mogg |url-access=registration |access-date=November 19, 2011}}</ref>
<ref>{{Cite book| chapter-url=https://open.lib.umn.edu/macroeconomics/chapter/17-1-the-great-depression-and-keynesian-economics/| title=17.1 the Great Depression and Keynesian Economics – Principles of Macroeconomics| chapter=17.1 the Great Depression and Keynesian Economics| date=December 2016| publisher=University of Minnesota Libraries Publishing edition, 2016. This edition adapted from a work originally produced in 2011 by a publisher who has requested that it not receive attribution.| archive-date=July 26, 2023| access-date=December 4, 2018| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230726224459/https://open.lib.umn.edu/macroeconomics/chapter/17-1-the-great-depression-and-keynesian-economics/| url-status=dead}}</ref>

====Banking crisis====
[[File:CPI 1914-2022.webp|thumb|alt=CPI 1914–2022|400px|
{{legend|#0076BA |[[Inflation]]}}
{{legend|#EE220C |[[Deflation]]}}
{{legend-line|#1DB100 solid 3px|[[Money supply|M2 money supply]] increases Year/Year}}
]]
In 1929–33 the economy was destabilized by bank failures. The initial reasons were substantial losses in investment banking, followed by [[bank run]]s. Bank runs occurred when a large number of customers lost confidence in their deposits (which were not insured) and rushed to withdraw their deposits. Runs destabilized a number of banks to the point where they faced bankruptcy. Between 1929 and 1933 40% of all banks (9,490 out of 23,697 banks) went bankrupt.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Hafer |first=Rik W. |url={{google books|plainurl=y|id=EuLEEAAAQBAJ|page=18}} |title=The Federal Reserve System: An Encyclopedia |date=2005-07-30 |publisher=Bloomsbury Publishing USA |isbn=978-0-313-06274-2 |language=en|page= 18}}</ref> Much of the [[Great Depression]]'s economic damage was caused directly by bank runs.<ref>[[Ben Bernanke]], "Nonmonetary effects of the financial crisis in the propagation of the Great Depression", (1983) ''American Economic Review''. Am 73#3 257–76.</ref>

Hoover had already considered a ''bank holiday'' to prevent further bank runs, but rejected the idea because he was afraid to trip a panic. Roosevelt acted as soon as he took office; he closed all the banks in the country and kept them all closed until he could pass new legislation.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.time.com/time/printout/0,8816,745289,00.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070930214931/http://www.time.com/time/printout/0,8816,745289,00.html|archive-date=September 30, 2007|title=Bottom |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |date=March 13, 1933|access-date=October 11, 2008}}</ref> On March 9, Roosevelt sent to Congress the [[Emergency Banking Act]], drafted in large part by Hoover's top advisors. The act was passed and signed into law the same day. It provided for a system of reopening sound banks under [[United States Department of the Treasury|Treasury]] supervision, with federal loans available if needed. Three-quarters of the banks in the [[Federal Reserve|Federal Reserve System]] reopened within the next three days. Billions of dollars in hoarded currency and gold flowed back into them within a month, thus stabilizing the banking system. By the end of 1933, 4,004 small local banks were permanently closed and merged into larger banks. Their deposits totaled $3.6 billion; depositors lost a total of $540 million, and eventually received on average 85 cents on the dollar of their deposits; it is a common myth that they received nothing back.<ref>{{cite book |author1=Milton Friedman |author2=Anna Jacobson Schwartz |title=A Monetary History of the United States, 1867–1960 |url={{google books | plainurl=y | id=Q7J_EUM3RfoC }}|year=1963 |publisher=Princeton University Press |isbn=978-0-691-00354-2 |pages=438–9}}</ref> The [[Glass–Steagall legislation|Glass–Steagall Act]] limited commercial bank securities activities and affiliations between commercial banks and securities firms to regulate speculations. It also established the [[Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation]] (FDIC), which insured deposits for up to $250,000, ending the risk of runs on banks.<ref>{{cite book |first=Susan E. |last=Kennedy |title=The Banking Crisis of 1933 |url=https://archive.org/details/bankingcrisisof10000kenn |url-access=registration |year=1973|publisher=University Press of Kentucky |isbn=978-0-8131-1285-5 }}</ref>

====Unemployment====
{{Main|Unemployment in the United States}}
Unemployment reached 25 percent in the worst days of 1932–33, but it was unevenly distributed. Job losses were less severe among women than men, among workers in nondurable industries (such as food and clothing), in services and sales, and in government jobs. The least skilled inner city men had much higher unemployment rates, as did young people who had a hard time getting their first job, and men over the age of 45 who if they lost their job would seldom find another one because employers had their choice of younger men. Millions were hired in the Great Depression, but men with weaker credentials were never hired, and fell into a long-term unemployment trap. The migration that brought millions of farmers and townspeople to the bigger cities in the 1920s suddenly reversed itself, as unemployment made the cities unattractive, and the network of kinfolk and more ample food supplies made it wise for many to go back.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Jensen |first=Richard J. |date=1989 |title=The Causes and Cures of Unemployment in the Great Depression |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/203954 |journal=The Journal of Interdisciplinary History |volume=19 |issue=4 |pages=553–583 |doi=10.2307/203954 |jstor=203954 |issn=0022-1953|url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Margo |first=Robert A. |date=1991 |title=The Microeconomics of Depression Unemployment |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/2122578 |journal=The Journal of Economic History |volume=51 |issue=2 |pages=333–341 |doi=10.1017/S0022050700038973 |jstor=2122578 |issn=0022-0507}}</ref>

[[Local government in the United States|City governments]] in 1930–31 tried to meet the depression by expanding public works projects, as president Herbert Hoover strongly encouraged. However tax revenues were plunging, and the cities as well as private relief agencies were totally overwhelmed by 1931 men were unable to provide significant additional relief. They fell back on the cheapest possible relief, soup kitchens which provided free meals for anyone who showed up.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Poppendieck |first1=Janet |url={{google books|plainurl=y|id=NtElDQAAQBAJ}}|title=Breadlines Knee-Deep in Wheat: Food Assistance in the Great Depression |last2=Nestle |first2=Marion |date=2014-04-26 |publisher=Univ of California Press |isbn=978-0-520-27753-3 |language=en}}</ref> After 1933 new sales taxes and infusions of federal money helped relieve the fiscal distress of the cities, but the budgets did not fully recover until 1941.

The federal programs launched by Hoover and greatly expanded by president Roosevelt's [[New Deal]] used massive construction projects to try to jump start the economy and solve the unemployment crisis. The alphabet agencies ERA, [[Civilian Conservation Corps|CCC]], [[Federal Emergency Relief Administration|FERA]], [[Works Progress Administration|WPA]] and [[Public Works Administration|PWA]] built and repaired the public infrastructure in dramatic fashion, but did little to foster the recovery of the private sector. FERA, CCC, and especially WPA focused on providing unskilled jobs for long-term unemployed men.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Winston |first1=Ellen |last2=Howard |first2=Donald S. |date=February 1944 |title=The WPA and Federal Relief Policy. |url=https://doi.org/10.2307/2086043 |journal=American Sociological Review |volume=9 |issue=1 |pages=121 |doi=10.2307/2086043 |jstor=2086043 |issn=0003-1224|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

====Relief====
{{Main|New Deal}}
The extent to which the spending for relief and public works provided a sufficient stimulus to revive the U.S. economy, or whether it harmed the economy, is also debated. If one defines economic health entirely by the gross domestic product, the U.S. had gotten back on track by 1934, and made a full recovery by 1936, but as Roosevelt said, one third of the nation was ill fed, ill-housed and ill-clothed. See Chart 3. GNP was 34% higher in 1936 than 1932, and 58% higher in 1940 on the eve of war. The economy grew 58% from 1932 to 1940 in 8 years of peacetime, and then grew another 56% from 1940 to 1945 in 5 years of wartime. The unemployment rate fell from 25.2% in 1932 to 13.9% in 1940 when the draft started. During the war the economy operated under so many different conditions that comparison is impossible with peacetime, such as massive spending, price controls, bond campaigns, controls over raw materials, prohibitions on new housing and new automobiles, rationing, guaranteed cost-plus profits, subsidized wages, and the draft of 12 million soldiers.

[[File:PIBUSA1920-40.svg|lang=en|thumb|Chart 3: GDP annual pattern and long-term trend, 1920–40, in billions of constant dollars<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Carter |first1=Susan B. |url={{google books | plainurl=y | id=4FqQNAEACAAJ }}|title=The Historical Statistics of the United States Online Edition: Millennial Edition -series Ca9|last2=Gartner |first2=Scott Sigmund |last3=Haines |first3=Michael R. |last4=Olmstead |first4=Alan L. |last5=Sutch |first5=Richard |last6=Wright |first6=Gavin |date=2006-05-01 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |isbn=978-0-511-13297-1 |language=en}}</ref>]]

In 1995 economist [[Robert Whaples]] stated that measuring the effect of the New Deal remains a thorny issue for economists because it is so difficult to measure the effects it had on the country. A survey of academic specialists by Whaples showed that in regards to the statement "The New Deal lengthened and deepened the Great Depression", 27% generally agreed, 22% agreed but with provisos, and 51% disagreed. Among professional historians, 6% agreed, 21% agreed with provisos, and 74% disagreed.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Bandyk |first=Matthew |title=Did the New Deal Work? |url=https://www.usnews.com/articles/business/economy/2008/04/11/did-the-new-deal-work.html |date=April 11, 2008 |magazine=[[U.S. News & World Report]] |access-date=June 17, 2024}}</ref> However, economist [[Eric Rauchway]] of the [[University of California]] stated "very few people disapprove of most of the New Deal reforms", which include [[Social Security (United States)|Social Security]], the [[U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission|Securities and Exchange Commission]], the [[Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation|Federal Deposit Insurance Corp.]], and [[Fannie Mae]]. Regardless, unemployment peaked in 1932 at 25% and was reduced to 13.9% by 1940.<ref>{{cite web|title=EH.R: FORUM: The Great Depression|url=http://eh.net/lists/archives/eh.res/feb-1997/0010.php|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080616030525/http://eh.net/lists/archives/eh.res/feb-1997/0010.php|archive-date=June 16, 2008|access-date=July 24, 2017|publisher=Eh.net}}</ref>

As Broadus Mitchell summarized, "Most indexes worsened until the summer of 1932, which may be called the low point of the depression economically and psychologically".<ref name=mitc/>{{rp| 404}} Economic indicators show the American economy declined until February 1933. After Roosevelt took office, there began a steady, sharp upward recovery that persisted until the brief [[Recession of 1937–1938]] (see graph) after which they continued their upward climb. Thus the Federal Reserve Index of Industrial Production bottomed at 52.8 on July 1, 1932, and was practically unchanged at 54.3 on March 1, 1933; however by July 1, 1933, it had climbed to 85.5 (with 1935–39 = 100, and for comparison 2005 = 1,342).<ref>[http://research.stlouisfed.org/fred2/data/INDPRO.txt Industrial Production Index] Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (US)</ref>

====New Deal impact====
A 2017 review of the published scholarship summarized the findings of researchers as follows:<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Fishback|first=Price|date=2017|title=How Successful Was the New Deal? The Microeconomic Impact of New Deal Spending and Lending Policies in the 1930s|journal=Journal of Economic Literature|volume=55|issue=4|pages=1435–1485|doi=10.1257/jel.20161054|s2cid=147049093|url=https://www2.warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/research/centres/cage/manage/publications/274-2016_fishback.pdf}}</ref>

<blockquote>The studies find that public works and relief spending had state income multipliers of around one, increased consumption activity, attracted internal migration, reduced crime rates, and lowered several types of mortality. The farm programs typically aided large farm owners but eliminated opportunities for share croppers, tenants, and farm workers. The [[Home Owners' Loan Corporation]]'s purchases and refinancing of troubled mortgages staved off drops in housing prices and home ownership rates at relatively low ex post cost to taxpayers. The [[Reconstruction Finance Corporation]]'s loans to banks and railroads appear to have had little positive impact, although the banks were aided when the RFC took ownership stakes.</blockquote>

===Wartime output and controls: 1940–1945===
{{Main|United States home front during World War II}}
[[File:Women aluminum shells wwii.jpg|thumb|Women making aluminum shells for the war in 1942]]
Unemployment dropped to 2%, relief programs largely ended, and the industrial economy grew rapidly to new heights as millions of people moved to new jobs in war centers, and 16 million men and 300,000 women were drafted or volunteered for military service.{{efn|Of the 16 million men, 10  million were drafted. Women were never drafted.}}

All economic sectors grew during the war. Farm output went from an index (by volume) of 106 in 1939 to 128 in 1943. Coal output went from 446 million tons in 1939 to 651 in 1943; oil from 1.3 billion barrels to 1.5 billion. Manufacturing output doubled, from a volume index of 109 in 1939 to 239 in 1943. Railroads strained to move it all to market, going from an output of 13.6 billion loaded car miles in 1939 to 23.3 in 1943.<ref>{{cite book|publisher=Bureau of the Census |title=Statistical Abstract of the United States: 1946 (The data use volume indexes not dollar amounts.)|year=1946 |pages= 507, 629, 749, 755, 811 }}</ref>

The [[War Production Board]] coordinated the nation's productive capabilities so that military priorities would be met. Converted consumer-products plants filled many military orders. Automakers built tanks and aircraft, for example, making the United States the "arsenal of democracy". In an effort to prevent rising national income and scarce consumer products from causing inflation, the newly created [[Office of Price Administration]] rationed and set prices for consumer items ranging from sugar to meat, clothing and gasoline, and otherwise tried to restrain price increases. It also set rent in war centers.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Vatter |first=Harold G. |url={{google books | plainurl=y | id=by-1HAAACAAJ }}|title=The U.S. Economy in World War II |date=1985 |publisher=Columbia University Press |isbn=978-0-231-05769-1 |language=en}}</ref>

Six million women took jobs in manufacturing and production; most were newly created temporary jobs in munitions. Some were replacing men away in the military. These working women were symbolized by the fictional character of [[Rosie the Riveter]]. After the war many women returned to household work as men returned from military service. The nation turned to the suburbs, as a pent-up demand for new housing was finally unleashed.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Campbell |first=D'Ann |url={{google books | plainurl=y | id=ASMqAAAAYAAJ }} |title=Women at War with America: Private Lives in a Patriotic Era |date=1984 |publisher=Harvard University Press |isbn=978-0-674-95475-5 |language=en}}</ref>

===Household gas, water, electricity, sanitation, heating, refrigeration===
By 1940 nearly 100% of urban homes had electricity, 80% had indoor flush toilets, 73% had gas heating or cooking, 58% central heating, 56% had mechanical refrigerators.{{r|Godron_2016|p=5}}

==Post-World War II prosperity: 1945–1973==
{{Further|Post–World War II economic expansion|History of the United States (1945–1964)|History of the United States (1964–1980)}}
[[File:Quarterly gross domestic product.png|center|800px|Quarterly gross domestic product]]
The period from the end of World War II to the early 1970s was a golden era of economic growth. $200&nbsp;billion in [[war bond]]s matured, and the [[G.I. Bill]] financed a well-educated workforce. The middle class swelled, as did GDP and productivity. This growth was distributed fairly evenly across the economic classes though not [[Racial pay gap in the United States|race]],<ref>{{Cite web|date=February 18, 2015|title=Today's racial wealth gap is wider than in the 1960s|url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/nation/todays-racial-wealth-gap-is-wider-than-in-the-1960s|access-date=January 29, 2022|website=PBS NewsHour|language=en-us}}</ref> which some attribute to the strength of labor unions in this period—labor union membership peaked historically in the U.S. during the 1950s, in the midst of this massive economic growth. Much of the growth came from the movement of low income farm workers into better paying jobs in the towns and cities—a process largely completed by 1960.

Congress created the [[Council of Economic Advisers|Council of Economic Advisors]], to promote high employment, high profits and low inflation. The [[Presidency of Dwight D. Eisenhower|Eisenhower administration]] (1953–1961) supported an activist contracyclical approach that helped to establish [[Keynesian economics|Keynesianism]] as a bipartisan economic policy for the nation. Especially important in formulating the CEA response to the recession—accelerating public works programs, easing credit, and reducing taxes—were [[Arthur F. Burns]] and [[Neil H. Jacoby]]. "I am now a Keynesian in economics", proclaimed Republican President [[Richard Nixon]] in 1969.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Reeves |first=Richard |url={{google books|plainurl=y|id=AdBdPvGxdaIC|page=295}} |title=President Nixon: Alone in the White House |date=2001-11-13 |publisher=Simon and Schuster |isbn=978-0-7432-2565-6 |language=en|page= 295}}</ref>
Although this period brought economic expansion to the country as a whole, it was not recession proof. The recessions of 1945, 1949, 1953, 1958, and 1960 saw a drastic decline in GDP.

The [[Mid-20th century baby boom|"Baby Boom"]] saw a dramatic increase in fertility in the period 1942–1957; it was caused by delayed marriages and childbearing during depression years, a surge in prosperity, a demand for suburban single-family homes (as opposed to inner city apartments) and new optimism about the future. The boom crested about 1957, then slowly declined.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Mintz |first1=Steven |url={{google books|plainurl=y|id=aqC_pjRKSWwC}}|title=Domestic Revolutions: A Social History Of American Family Life |last2=Kellogg |first2=Susan |date=1989-04-03 |publisher=Simon and Schuster |isbn=978-1-4391-0510-8 |language=en|chapter=9}}</ref>

===Agriculture===
====Farm machinery, fertilizer and high yield seed varieties====
Ammonia from plants built during World War II to make explosives became available for making fertilizers, leading to a permanent decline in real fertilizer prices.<ref>{{cite book|title=A Great Leap Forward: 1930s Depression and U.S. Economic Growth|last=Field|first= Alexander |year= 2011 |publisher =Yale University Press|location= New Haven, London|isbn=978-0-300-15109-1 |pages=22–3}}</ref> The early 1950s was the peak period for tractor sales in the U.S. as the few remaining horses and mules were phased out. The horsepower of farm machinery underwent a large expansion.<ref name="Tractors">{{Cite web|last= White|first= William J.|title = Economic History of Tractors in the United States
|url =http://eh.net/encyclopedia/article/white.tractors.history.us|archive-url  =https://web.archive.org/web/20131024041712/http://eh.net/encyclopedia/article/white.tractors.history.us|archive-date = October 24, 2013
}}</ref> A successful cotton picking machine was introduced in 1949. The machine could do the work of 50 men picking by hand.<ref>{{cite book| author = Rifkin, Jeremy | title = The End of Work: The Decline of the Global Labor Force and the Dawn of the Post-Market Era | url  =https://archive.org/details/endofwork00jere | url-access = registration | publisher = Putnam Publishing Group | year = 1995 | isbn = 978-0-87477-779-6}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | url=http://mjperry.blogspot.com/2011/11/corn-yields-have-increased-six-times.html | title=Carpe Diem: U.S. Corn Yields Have Increased Six Times Since the 1930s and Are Estimated to Double by 2030}}</ref>

Research on plant breeding produced varieties of grain crops that could produce high yields with heavy fertilizer input. This resulted in the [[Green Revolution|Green revolution]], beginning in the 1940s.<ref>{{cite book |title= Enriching the Earth: Fritz Haber, Carl Bosch, and the Transformation of World Food Production|last=Smil|first=Vaclav |year=2004 |publisher= MIT Press|isbn= 978-0-262-69313-4 }}</ref> By the century's end yields of corn (maize) rose by a factor of over four. Wheat and soybean yields also rose significantly.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://quickstats.nass.usda.gov/?long_desc__LIKE=Corn&x=20&y=6|title=USDA/NASS QuickStats Ad-hoc Query Tool|website=quickstats.nass.usda.gov|access-date=December 6, 2019|archive-date=December 6, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191206163128/https://quickstats.nass.usda.gov/%3Flong_desc__LIKE%3DCorn%26x%3D20%26y%3D6|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.agry.purdue.edu/ext/corn/news/timeless/yieldtrends.html|title=Historical Corn Grain Yields for the U.S. (Purdue University)|website=www.agry.purdue.edu}}</ref>

====Government policies====
{{main|United States farm bill}}
The New Deal era farm programs were continued into the 1940s and 1950s, with the goal of supporting the prices received by farmers. Typical programs involved farm loans, commodity subsidies, and price supports.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Zulauf |first1=Carl |last2=Orden |first2=David |year=2016 |title=80 Years of Farm Bills—Evolutionary Reform |url=http://www.choicesmagazine.org/UserFiles/file/cmsarticle_551.pdf|journal=[[Choices (journal)|Choices]] |volume=31 |issue=4 |pages=1–2 |issn=0886-5558}}</ref> The rapid decline in the farm population led to a smaller voice in Congress. So the well-organized Farm Bureau and other lobbyists, worked in the 1970s to appeal to urban Congressman through food stamp programs for the poor. By 2000, the food stamp program was the largest component of the farm bill. In 2010, the [[Tea Party movement]] brought in a number of Republicans committed to cutting all federal subsidies, including those in agriculture. Meanwhile, urban Democrats strongly opposed reductions, pointing to the severe hardships caused by the 2008–10 economic recession. The [[Agricultural Act of 2014]] saw many rural Republican Congressman voting against the program despite its support from farmers; it passed with urban support.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Bosso |first=Christopher J. |url={{google books | plainurl=y | id=4OOiEAAAQBAJ }} |title=Framing the Farm Bill: Interests, Ideology, and Agricultural Act of 2014 |date=2017-03-17 |publisher=University Press of Kansas |isbn=978-0-7006-2420-1 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=The US Agricultural Act of 2014: Overview and analysis discussion paper 01393|first1=Carl |last1=Zulauf |first2= David |last2=Orden|url=https://ebrary.ifpri.org/digital/collection/p15738coll2/id/128802/ |year=2014|access-date=December 7, 2024 |website=ebrary.ifpri.org |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |first1=Carl |last1=Zulauf |first2= David |last2=Orden|date=April 1, 2016 |title=Political Economy of the 2014 Farm Bill |journal=American Journal of Agricultural Economics |volume=97|issue=5 |pages=1298–1311|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160401131433/http://ajae.oxfordjournals.org/content/97/5/1298.full |url=http://ajae.oxfordjournals.org/content/97/5/1298.full |archive-date=April 1, 2016|access-date=2024-12-05 }}</ref>

===Aircraft and air transportation industries===
{{Main|Air transportation in the United States}}
Air transport was a major beneficiary of the war. The United States was the leading producer of combat aircraft during World War II and had a large surplus of machine tools and manufacturing facilities for airplanes at the end of the war.{{r|Hounshell1984}} There were also experienced airplane manufacturing and maintenance personnel. Additionally, radar had been developed just before the war.

The aircraft industry had the highest productivity growth of any major industry, growing by 8.9% per year in 1929–1966.<ref name="Rosenberg-1982">{{cite book |title= Inside the Black Box: Technology and Economics |last= Rosenberg |first= Nathan |year= 1982 |publisher= Cambridge University Press |location= Cambridge, New York |isbn= 978-0-521-27367-1 |page= [https://archive.org/details/insideblackboxte00rose/page/164 164] |url =https://archive.org/details/insideblackboxte00rose/page/164 }}</ref>

During World War II the United States hired hundreds of thousands of workers, put them all in 4 major factories and had a government budget of over $3 billion ({{Inflation|index=US|value=3000000000|start_year=1943|end_year=2019|fmt=eq|cursign=$}}). The [[Boeing B-29 Superfortress|B-29]] project required the [[United States Army Air Forces|US Army Air Forces]] to have unprecedented organizational capabilities as this project included several major private contractors and labor unions. American aircraft production was the single largest sector of the war economy, costing $45 billion (almost a quarter of the $183 billion spent on war production), employing a staggering two million workers, and, most importantly, producing over 125,000 aircraft. Production of Selected U.S. Military Aircraft (1941–1945): Bombers-49,123 Fighters-63,933 Cargo-14,710 Total-127,766.{{citation needed|date=July 2021}}

===Housing===
{{Main|Housing in the United States}}
Very little housing had been built during the Great Depression and World War II, except for emergency quarters near war industries. Overcrowded and inadequate apartments was the common condition. Some suburbs had developed around large cities where there was rail transportation to the jobs downtown. However, the real growth in suburbia depended on the availability of automobiles, highways, and inexpensive housing. The population had grown, and the stock of family savings had accumulated the money for down payments, automobiles and appliances. The product was a great housing boom. Whereas an average of 316,000 new housing non-farm units had been constructed from the 1930s through 1945, there were 1,450,000 units built annually from 1946 through 1955.<ref>U.S. Bureau of the Census, ''Historical Statistics of the United States '' (1976) series H-156</ref>

The [[G.I. Bill|G.I. Bill of Rights]] guaranteed low cost loans for veterans, with low down payments, and low interest rates. With 16 million eligible veterans, the opportunity to buy a house was suddenly at hand. In 1947 alone, 540,000 veterans bought one; their average price was $7300. The construction industry kept prices low by standardization – for example standardizing sizes for kitchen cabinets, refrigerators and stoves, allowed for mass production of kitchen furnishings. Developers purchased empty land just outside the city, installed tract houses based on a handful of designs, and provided streets and utilities, or local public officials race to build schools.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Goulden |first=Joseph C. |url={{google books|plainurl=y|id=iLK8DwAAQBAJ}}|title=The Best Years, 1945-1950 |date=2019-12-18 |publisher=Courier Dover Publications |isbn=978-0-486-83826-7 |language=en}}</ref>{{rp|135–39}} The most famous development was [[Levittown, New York|Levittown]], in [[Long Island]] just east of New York City. It offered a new house for $1000 down, and $70 a month; it featured three bedrooms, fireplace, gas range and gas furnace, and a landscaped lot of 75 by 100 feet, all for a total price of $10,000. Veterans could get one with a much lower down payment.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Kelly |first=Barbara M. |url={{google books | plainurl=y | id=cSUuqae8oIYC }}|title=Expanding the American Dream: Building and Rebuilding Levittown |date=1993-01-01 |publisher=SUNY Press |isbn=978-0-7914-1287-9 |language=en}}</ref>

===Interstate highway system===
{{Main|Interstate Highway System}}
Construction of the [[Interstate Highway System]] began in 1956 under [[Presidency of Dwight D. Eisenhower|President Eisenhower]]. In long-term perspective the interstate highway system was a remarkable success, that has done much to sustain Eisenhower's positive reputation. Although there have been objections to the negative impact of clearing neighborhoods in cities, the system has been well received. The railroad system for passengers and freight declined sharply, but the trucking expanded dramatically and the cost of shipping and travel fell sharply. [[Suburbanization]] became widespread, with the rapid growth of easily accessible, larger, cheaper housing than was available in the overcrowded central cities. Tourism dramatically expanded as well, creating a demand for more service stations, motels, restaurants and visitor attractions. There was much more long-distance movement to the [[Sun Belt|Sunbelt]] for winter vacations, or for permanent relocation, with convenient access to visits to relatives back home. In rural areas, towns and small cities off the grid lost out as shoppers followed the interstate, and new factories were located near them.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Blas |first=Elisheva |date=2010 |title=The Dwight D. Eisenhower National System of Interstate and Defense Highways: The Road to Success? |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/25799401 |journal=The History Teacher |volume=44 |issue=1 |pages=127–142 |jstor=25799401 |issn=0018-2745}}</ref>

===Computer technology===
{{Main|Computer}}
Mainframe business computer systems were introduced in the 1950s following the manufacture of [[transistor]]s. Mainframe computers
were in widespread use by the 1960s. These computers handled a variety of accounting, billing and payroll applications.

One highly significant application was the [[Sabre (computer system)|Sabre airline reservations system]], which first went into operation in 1960. With Sabre reservations could be placed remotely using teleprinters and all functions were done automatically, including ticket printing. This eliminated manually handling file cards.

===Fiscal policy===
Federal taxes on incomes, profits and payrolls had risen to high levels during World War II and had been cut back only slowly; the highest rates for individuals reached the 90% level. Congress cut tax rates in 1964. President [[Lyndon B. Johnson]] (1963–69) dreamed of creating a "[[Great Society]]", and began a number of new social programs to that end, such as [[Medicaid]] and [[Medicare (United States)|Medicare]]. During the [[Korean War]], the [[Economic Stabilization Agency]] supervised [[price controls]].<ref>{{cite book |last1=Pierpaoli |first1=Paul G. |title=Truman and Korea: The Political Culture of the Early Cold War |date=1999 |publisher=University of Missouri Press |isbn=978-0-8262-6131-1 |page=37 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=eB38Ip9xRZwC&q=%22%20%20Economic%20Stabilization%20Agency%22%20price%20controls |language=en}}</ref>

===Military and space spending===
After the [[Cold War]] began in 1947, and especially after the [[Korean War]] began in 1950, the government adopted a strategy in [[NSC 68]] military spending.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Young |first=Ken |date=2013 |title=Revisiting NSC 68 |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/26924304 |journal=Journal of Cold War Studies |volume=15 |issue=1 |pages=3–33 |doi=10.1162/JCWS_a_00306 |jstor=26924304 |issn=1520-3972|url-access=subscription }}</ref> Economists examined how much this "military Keynesianism" stimulated the economy.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Mintz |first1=Alex |last2=Hicks |first2=Alexander |date=1984 |title=Military Keynesianism in the United States, 1949-1976: Disaggregating Military Expenditures and Their Determination |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/2779222 |journal=American Journal of Sociology |volume=90 |issue=2 |pages=411–417 |doi=10.1086/228086 |jstor=2779222 |issn=0002-9602|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

President Eisenhower feared that excessive military spending would damage the economy, so he downsized the Army after Korea and shifted priorities to missiles and nuclear weapons (which were much less expensive than army divisions). He also promoted the [[Interstate Highway System|Interstate Highway]] system as necessary for national defense, and made space exploration a priority. His successor [[John F. Kennedy]] made a crewed mission to the moon a national priority. Much of the new spending went to California and the West, a continuation of wartime spending.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Fernlund |first=Kevin J. |url={{google books|plainurl=y|id=eyMjAQAAIAAJ}}|title=The Cold War American West, 1945-1989 |date=1998 |publisher=University of New Mexico Press |isbn=978-0-8263-1984-5 |language=en}}</ref>

An even greater impact came in the South, where it stimulated a modernization of the economy away from cotton towards manufacturing and high technology. For example, there were new, large technologically sophisticated installations at the Atomic Energy Commission's [[Savannah River Site]] in South Carolina; the [[Redstone Arsenal]] at [[Huntsville, Alabama]]; nuclear research facilities at [[Oak Ridge National Laboratory|Oak Ridge, Tennessee]]; and space facilities at [[Space Coast|Cape Canaveral]], Florida, at the [[Johnson Space Center|Lyndon B. Johnson Space Center]] in Houston, and at the [[Stennis Space Center|John C. Stennis Space Center]] in Mississippi.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Frederickson |first=Kari |url={{google books|plainurl=y|id=PNY4FRLGamsC}}|title=Cold War Dixie: Militarization and Modernization in the American South |date=2013-06-01 |publisher=University of Georgia Press |isbn=978-0-8203-4519-2 |language=en}}</ref>

The Defense Department financed some of private industry's research and development throughout these decades, most notably [[ARPANET]] (which would become the Internet).

===Decline of labor unions===

The percentage of workers belonging to a union (or "density") in the United States peaked in 1954 at almost 35% and the total number of union members peaked in 1979 at an estimated 21.0 million.<ref>{{Cite web|title=American unions membership declines as public support fluctuates|url=https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2014/02/20/for-american-unions-membership-trails-far-behind-public-support/|access-date=April 26, 2021|website=Pew Research Center|date=February 20, 2014 |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":4b">{{Cite journal|last=Mayer|first=Brian|date=2009|title=Cross-Movement Coalition Formation: Bridging the Labor-Environment Divide*|url=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1475-682X.2009.00286.x|journal=Sociological Inquiry|language=en|volume=79|issue=2|pages=219–239|doi=10.1111/j.1475-682X.2009.00286.x|issn=1475-682X|url-access=subscription}}</ref> Union membership has continued to decline into the 2010s due to [[right-to-work law]]s adopted by a number of states, [[globalization]] undermining higher-wage firms, and increasing political opposition exemplified by the breaking of the 1981 strike of the [[Professional Air Traffic Controllers Organization (1968)]] by President [[Ronald Reagan]].

