===Great Lockdown and aftermath (2019–present)===
{{main|Economic impact of the COVID-19 pandemic in the United States}}

On September 16, 2019, the Federal Reserve announced that it would begin acting as the role of investor in order to provide funds in the [[Repurchase agreement|repo markets]] after the overnight lending rate jumped above 8% due to a series of technical factors that limited the supply of available funds.<ref name= "Fed_MPI">{{Cite web|url=https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20191011a.htm|title=Statement Regarding Monetary Policy Implementation|work= [[Federal Reserve]]|date = October 11, 2019}}</ref> Five months after the decision was made, the American stock markets [[2020 stock market crash|suffered their biggest crash]] in modern U.S. history as a result of concerns surrounding the [[COVID-19 pandemic|coronavirus pandemic]] and the [[2020 Russia–Saudi Arabia oil price war|Russia-Saudi Arabia oil price war]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/money/2020/02/27/dow-markets-plunge-correction-coronavirus/4889977002/ |title=Dow plunges 1,191 points, its biggest one-day point drop, as coronavirus fears escalate |last=Menton |first=Jessica |date=February 27, 2020 |website=USA Today |language=en-US |access-date=March 12, 2020}}</ref> Before the crash happened, the [[Unemployment in the United States|unemployment rate]] in the United States stood at 3.6% in late 2019, which was the lowest unemployment rate since [[World War II]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://research.stlouisfed.org/fred2/series/UNRATE|title=Fred Database-Unemployment Rate|website=Research.stlouisfed.org|access-date=October 4, 2017|date=January 1948}}</ref> Although the unemployment rate decreased substantially between 2009 and 2019, [[Income inequality in the United States|income inequality]] continued to increase. In September 2019, the [[United States Census Bureau]] reported that income inequality in the United States had reached its highest level in 50 years, with the [[Gini coefficient|Gini index]] increasing from 48.2 in 2017 to 48.5 in 2018.<ref>{{cite news |last= Schneider|first=Mike|date=September 26, 2019 |title=Income inequality grew again: The highest level in more than 50 years, Census Bureau says|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/money/2019/09/26/income-inequality-highest-over-50-years-census-bureau-shows/3772919002/|work=[[USA Today]] |access-date=December 8, 2019}}</ref> Despite the low unemployment, the [[ISM Report On Business|ISM Manufacturing Index]] dropped below 50% in August 2019, reaching a record low of 48.3% in October of that year (which was the lowest level since June 2009); it would continue to remain below 50% in the months leading up to the crash.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.instituteforsupplymanagement.org/news/NewsReleaseDetail.cfm?ItemNumber=31158&SSO=1|title=October 2019 Manufacturing ISM Report on Business|work=[[Institute for Supply Management]]|date=November 1, 2019|access-date=May 20, 2020|archive-date=June 10, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200610155739/https://www.instituteforsupplymanagement.org/news/NewsReleaseDetail.cfm?ItemNumber=31158&SSO=1}}</ref>

